# Instructions for Claude / Main Developer

We have implemented and deployed X posting integration to the pipeline, along with the safety guard to skip manually-triggered components.

To make X posting work in production, you need to perform the following:

## 1. Set the X Session Token Secret
The X client requires a session token cookie string encoded via `rettiwt-api`'s AuthService. Run this to encode and set the secret:

```bash
# Get cookie string (auth_token=...; ct0=...; twid=...) from browser
node -e "const { AuthService } = require('rettiwt-api'); console.log(AuthService.encodeCookie('YOUR_COOKIE_STRING'));"

# Take the encoded token and set it in Wrangler:
npx wrangler secret put X_SESSION_TOKEN
```

## 2. Test X Posting
Once the secret is set, any organic run through the listener -> extract -> verify-workflow pipeline that reaches a terminal status will post a reply back to X.

To test this manually with a simulated tweet without relying on organic polling:
1. Insert a mock component and a mock source post in the D1 database representing a real tweet ID you want to reply to.
2. Run the `verification-workflow` on it.
3. The workflow will read the post ID and call the X client to reply to it.
